<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Artuqids of Hasankeyf</h1><p class="st-meta">1102 CE – 1232 CE · capital Hasankeyf</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkmen dynasty on the Tigris that ruled Hasankeyf and Amid and employed the engineer al-Jazari, author of a celebrated book of machines.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuq-ibn-ekseb">Artuq</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokmen-ibn-artuq">Sokmen</a>, who lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 1098, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hisn Kayfa</a> (Hasankeyf) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in 1102 and helped defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> in 1104.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a> line ruled the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> valley and from 1183, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> gave them Amid, the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a>. They built the great bridge at Hasankeyf and repaired the walls of Amid. At their court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-al-jazari">Ibn al-Razzaz al-Jazari</a> completed around 1206 his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-knowledge-of-ingenious-mechanical-devices">Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices</a>, describing water clocks, automata and pumps. Their coins often carried figurative images borrowed from Byzantine and classical models.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Caught between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, the dynasty lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf">Hasankeyf</a> and Amid to the Ayyubid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> in 1232, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ayyubids-of-hisn-kayfa">Ayyubids of Hisn Kayfa</a> took over.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/artuqids-of-hasankeyf.jpg" alt="Artuqids of Hasankeyf" width="320" height="221"><figcaption>Image: Cenk847 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artuqidsmap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1102 CE – 1232 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hasankeyf</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sökmen ibn Artuq, Kara Arslan, Nur al-Din Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Artuqids of Hisn Kayfa and Amid, Artuqid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1167&amp;focus=artuqids-of-hasankeyf" aria-label="Artuqids of Hasankeyf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5714 821 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Artuqids of Hasankeyf lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6034 1077L6481 1127M6034 1077L5811 1337M6034 1077L6003 1071M6034 1077L6034 1077"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M5811 1337h0M6003 1071h0M6034 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6034 1077h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6034 1077h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hasankeyf. <a href="/atlas?year=1167&amp;focus=artuqids-of-hasankeyf">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
